{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0db6388e25364b030169bb2e551eb88191a97c7829d0ef4def", "created": 1773874773, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Stress, Supportive Care, and Quality of Life in Cancer Care Decision-Making\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on how patients’ stress during cancer-care decision-making connects to supportive care goals and broader quality-of-life (QoL) outcomes. Decision-making about cancer care is explicitly described as stressful, linking the decision process to the stress state. Stress reduction is explicitly framed as a supportive-care goal alongside cancer control and QoL. QoL itself is defined and operationalized through a patient’s ability to participate in regular activities and through valued life domains such as being with friends and family, while minimizing cognitive problems is also stated as a treatment goal tied to enjoying life and successful outcomes beyond cancer reduction. Overall, the network describes an interrelated framework: stressful decisions can elevate stress, supportive care aims to lower stress, and QoL is evaluated through daily functioning and lived experience (activities, relationships, cognition). [Data: Entities (899, 900, 3219, 3249, 4354,+more); Relationships (1353, 9139, 6404, 9145, 9143)]\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"Decision-making about cancer care is explicitly linked to stress\",\"explanation\":\"The community contains an explicit connection between the process of choosing among cancer care options and the experience of stress. Decision-making is characterized as involving complex choices “under pressure,” with the text stating it can be stressful. [Data: Entities (899, 900); Relationships (1353)]\\n\\nThis linkage positions stress not merely as a background condition but as an expected psychological burden during key clinical moments (e.g., weighing