to the stress-inducing process of making tough care choices. [Data: Entities (3219, 899, 900)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Participation in regular activities is a specific component used to evaluate quality of life\",\"explanation\":\"The community specifies that QoL is defined in part by a person’s ability to participate in regular activities. Symptoms are assessed by how much they affect an individual’s capacity to take part in those activities, making daily participation a concrete operational measure of QoL in this framework. [Data: Entities (3219, 3249); Relationships (6404)]\\n\\nThis relationship identifies a functional pathway for understanding and monitoring QoL: changes in symptoms and health status can be interpreted through impacts on routine life. As a result, interventions aimed at improving QoL can be grounded in restoring or maintaining participation in regular activities. [Data: Entities (3219, 3249); Relationships (6404)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Friends and family are explicitly included as part of quality-of-life goals during treatment\",\"explanation\":\"Being with friends and family is explicitly listed as part of QoL goals during treatment. This places close relationships and social connection within the QoL construct, not merely as contextual factors but as stated treatment-related aims associated with enjoying life. [Data: Entities (3219, 4354); Relationships (9145)]\\n\\nIn the broader community structure, this provides a social domain for QoL that complements the functional domain (regular activities). Together, they reinforce that QoL is multi-dimensional: it includes what patients can do day-to-day and who they can be with and enjoy life alongside. [Data: Entities (3219, 3249, 4354); Relationships (6404, 9145)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Minimizing cognitive problems is presented as a treatment goal tied to quality of life and enjoying life\",\"explanation\":\"The network states that minimizing cognitive problems is explicitly listed as a goal tied to enjoying life and successful treatment beyond cancer reduction, connecting cognition to QoL outcomes. This frames cognitive effects as a quality-of-life issue that clinical goals may seek to reduce. [Data: Entities (4349, 3219); Relationships (9143)]\\n\\nThis relationship adds a cognitive dimension to QoL alongside functional participation and social connection. It also indicates that “successful treatment” is not limited to disease control in this community’s framing; maintaining cognitive well-being is part of the articulated objective set connected to QoL. [Data: Entities (4349, 3219); Relationships (9143)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community emphasizes a patient-centered model where supportive care, stress, and QoL reinforce one another\",\"explanation\":\"Across the relationships, stress is connected to the decision-making process, and stress reduction is explicitly tied to supportive care goals that complement QoL and cancer control. This creates a coherent model: decision pressure can elevate stress, and supportive care explicitly aims to lower stress in parallel with improving QoL. [Data: Entities (899, 900, 3219); Relationships (1353, 9139)]\\n\\nQoL is then grounded in measurable lived outcomes (regular activities) and valued experiences (friends and family), with cognitive problems named as a QoL issue to minimize. Together, these elements form an interlinked community describing how care can address psychological strain and preserve daily function, relationships, and cognition as part of treatment success. [Data: Entities (3219, 3249, 4354, 4349, 900,+more); Relationships (6404, 9145, 9143, 9139)]\"}],\"rating\":3.2,\"rating_explanation\":\"The impact severity is low-to-moderate because the community describes clinically important but non-malicious patient-care factors (stress, supportive care, and quality-of-life domains) that can materially affect treatment experience and outcomes.\"}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_spec